# question: Which clinical alarms coincide with a technical condition that reduces their validity?
# rationale: Tests, using a structural proxy (a signal-quality fault
#   co-located with a metric from the same signal), whether a coinciding
#   technical condition can be recognised as reducing a clinical alarm's
#   validity.
#
# Distinct from CAT1's a/b rules (deferred -- those need temporal ordering
# and asystole-specific reasoning, not yet built). This CQ only asks
# whether the underlying REPRESENTATION can express "reduces validity" at
# all -- answer: only as a structural proxy, not a real semantic. The
# framework has no mda:invalidates (or similar) relation. What's detectable
# is: a signal-quality fault (hasQualityState != Good) coinciding with a
# metric reading on the SAME sensing pathway (same Signal particular that
# the metric's SignalAnalysis is analyzedBy) -- treating that structural
# co-location as standing in for "reduces validity", which is an inference
# the query makes, not something the ontology asserts.
#
# "Clinical alarm" operationalised precisely, not read off the question's
# natural-language wording: an alarm with mda:hasCategory
# alarmcat:Physiological (see ontology.ttl's hasCategory declaration,
# range skos:Concept over alarmcat:Scheme).
#
# CORRECTED after a false-positive was found and reproduced (a synthetic
# 3-alarm case: PHILIPSMONITOR - SpO2 laag + PHILIPSMONITOR - HF hoog +
# PHILIPSMONITOR - Storing in SpO2, same device) -- an earlier version of
# this query only checked (a) SOME alarmcat:Physiological alarm is present
# in the tick, and (b) SOMEWHERE in the tick, a rate-bearing metric's
# analysis sits behind a non-Good-quality signal. These were two
# independent existence checks with no join between them, so ANY
# physiological alarm present got credited with ANY quality fault present
# in the same tick, regardless of pathway -- "HF hoog" (ECG pathway) was
# wrongly flagged as invalidated by a fault on the unrelated SpO2 pathway.
# Not caught by the fabricated cq9_pos/cq9_neg pair, which only ever has
# exactly one physiological + one technical alarm per patient.
#
# Fixed by joining through mda:triggeredByStructure (ontology.ttl,
# op_knowledge.py's alarm_message()/ground_chain()): every alarm message
# now carries an edge to the specific technical particular (Sensor/Signal/
# SignalAnalysis/Metric) that alarm's OWN archetype terminates at --
# ground_chain's "leaves" (see its own docstring). ?clinicalAlarm's own
# message's anchor is bound as ?myMetric, and ?analysis/?signal are derived
# FROM it (mda:producesMetric's inverse, then mda:analyzedBy's inverse),
# not independently matched elsewhere in the tick. This structurally rules
# out crediting one alarm's pathway with another, unrelated alarm's fault --
# on any device, not just the same-device case the false positive was found
# on. Deliberately requires ?clinicalAlarm's own anchor to BE a Metric
# (?myMetric mda:hasRate ?rate): a physiological alarm whose own archetype
# never resolves to a measurement has no validity to reduce in the first
# place, so excluding it here is the correct scope, not a gap.
#
# Deferred, not added here: mda:hasTherapyDeliveryQuality (added to
# ontology.ttl/inference.ttl in a separate ontology update) is a second,
# REAL (non-proxy) technical-invalidity mechanism: a device-wide "what's
# being delivered is compromised" fact, entailed via a propertyChainAxiom
# from a metric:VentilationCircuitLeak reading, not merely a structural
# co-location proxy the way hasQualityState is used above. It is not yet
# tagged mda:situational true in ontology.ttl, though, so its entailments
# never reach this snapshot export -- confirmed empirically: fires
# correctly in the full OWL-RL closure (owlrl.DeductiveClosure), absent
# from clinical_context()'s output (0 triples vs. 1 in the untagged
# _extract_all view, tested against a synthetic ABox). Adding a UNION
# branch for it now would validate against an empty result for the wrong
# reason -- an ontology-level tagging fix, plus a new fabricated
# positive/negative pair, needed first. Blocked on that decision, not on
# this query.
#
# reasoning: required (runs against kg_inference.trig-style snapshot output
# from the operational layer -- CODE/evaluation_poc/core/op_knowledge.py --
# which already reasons per tick; this query performs no further OWL-RL
# itself)
#
# Graph-shape gotcha, worth documenting since it cost real debugging time:
# a snapshot named graph's OWN metadata (snap:forPatient, snap:atTime,
# rdf:type snap:Snapshot) is asserted via ds.add(...) into the DEFAULT
# graph -- describing the named graph as a resource -- NOT via ng.add(...)
# inside the named graph itself (see op_inference.py/build_trig scripts).
# `?g snap:forPatient ?patient` must therefore sit OUTSIDE any `GRAPH ?g
# {...}` block, or it silently never matches.
#
# Similarly, the technical chain up to and including SignalAnalysis
# (hasFunctionalUnit/hasSensor/sensorProducesSignal/analyzedBy) lives in
# the ACCUMULATED BACKGROUND (default graph) -- these nodes are
# Individuated/structural, timeless composition of the device -- while
# mda:Metric (Stateful) and its producesMetric link, plus every
# leaf_properties value (hasRate, hasQualityState, hasSensorOperationState,
# ...), are CONDITION, scoped to the specific tick's named graph. A query
# spanning from Signal to Metric must therefore split the property path at
# that boundary: the analyzedBy hop unscoped (default graph), the
# producesMetric hop inside GRAPH ?g. mda:triggeredByStructure itself is
# CONDITION-scoped too (asserted as part of the alarm message, which is
# only present in the tick's named graph while that alarm is active), so it
# stays inside GRAPH ?g alongside producesMetric.
#
# Validated against a fabricated positive/negative pair (DATA/CQ_evaluation/
# events_data.csv, patients cq9_pos/cq9_neg) before finalising: cq9_pos
# (PHILIPSMONITOR - SpO2 laag + PHILIPSMONITOR - Storing in SpO2, same
# device, same PulseOximeter pathway) fires, with ?clinicalLabel correctly
# resolving to "PHILIPSMONITOR - SpO2 laag" only; cq9_neg (the same SpO2
# alarm coinciding with an unrelated CARDIOHELP connection-loss alarm on a
# different device/pathway) correctly still does not fire. Re-validated
# against the synthetic 3-alarm counter-example that exposed the original
# bug: "HF hoog" no longer appears as ?clinicalLabel, only "SpO2 laag"
# does.

PREFIX mda: <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>
PREFIX snap: <https://w3id.org/mda/snapshot/>
PREFIX qualitystate: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/quality-state/>
PREFIX alarmcat: <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/alarm-category/>

SELECT ?patient ?g ?clinicalLabel
       (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT ?label; separator=" | ") AS ?alarms) WHERE {
  ?g snap:forPatient ?patient .
  GRAPH ?g {
    ?clinicalAlarm mda:hasLabel ?clinicalLabel ;
                    mda:hasCategory alarmcat:Physiological ;
                    mda:hasMessage ?clinicalMsg .
    ?clinicalMsg mda:triggeredByStructure ?myMetric .
    ?myMetric mda:hasRate ?rate .
    ?alarm mda:hasLabel ?label .
    ?signal mda:hasQualityState ?quality .
    FILTER(?quality != qualitystate:Good)
    ?analysis mda:producesMetric ?myMetric .
  }
  ?signal mda:analyzedBy ?analysis .
}
GROUP BY ?patient ?g ?clinicalLabel
ORDER BY ?patient
